s="\"SIAM J. Discrete Math. (https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/168183/7/WRAP-letter-graphs-geometric-grid-classes-permutations-2022.pdf)\\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 4.0 years ago; ** We uncover a connection between two seemingly unrelated notions: lettericity, from structural graph theory, and geometric griddability, from the world of permutation patterns. ... This is indeed the case with the two notions studied in the present paper: lettericity on the one hand, and geometric griddability on the other.\\nSIAM J. Discrete Math.  \\nVol. 36, No. 4, pp. 2774–2797  \\n© 2022 Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics\\n\\n# LETTER GRAPHS AND GEOMETRIC GRID CLASSES OF PERMUTATIONS*\\n\\nBOGDAN ALECU†, ROBERT FERGUSON‡, MAMADOU MOUSTAPHA KANTÉ§,  \\nVADIM V. LOZIN†, VINCENT VATTER‡, AND VICTOR ZAMARAEV¶\\n\\n**Abstract.** We uncover a connection between two seemingly unrelated notions: lettericity, from structural graph theory, and geometric griddability, from the world of permutation patterns. Both of these notions capture important structural properties of their respective classes of objects. We prove that these notions are equivalent in the sense that a permutation class is geometrically griddable if and only if the corresponding class of inversion graphs has bounded lettericity.\\n\\n**Key words.** inversion graphs, permutation patterns, well-quasi-order\\n\\n**MSC codes.** 05C75, 05A05, 68R10\\n\\n**DOI.** 10.1137/21M1449646\\n\\n## 1. Introduction.\\n\\nStructural graph theory and the study of permutation patterns are two flourishing areas of mathematics. They share a few conceptual similarities; among others, the widespread featuring of forbidden substructures, and the prominent usage of decompositions. Nevertheless, the fields have developed their own separate terminologies, techniques, and goals.\\n\\nBecause of this, it happens periodically that equivalent notions appear in the two areas, independently of each other, and under different names. In most cases, a relationship between such notions can be derived directly from their definitions. Sometimes, however, this link can be much more subtle. This is indeed the case with the two notions studied in the present paper: lettericity on the one hand, and geometric griddability on the other. At a first reading of the definitions, nothing suggests that there should be a connection between these two notions. However, such a connection does exist, and was first observed by Alecu, Lozin, de Werra, and Zamaraev [6, 7]. They showed that if \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}\\\\) is a geometrically griddable permutation class, then \\\\(\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\mathcal{C}}\\\\), the associated class of inversion graphs, has bounded lettericity. They conjectured that the converse to this statement also holds. We prove this conjecture, yielding the following result.\\n\\n**Theorem 1.1.** *The permutation class \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}\\\\) is geometrically griddable if and only if the corresponding graph class \\\\(\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\mathcal{C}}\\\\) has bounded lettericity.*\\n\\n*Received by the editors September 29, 2021; accepted for publication (in revised form) May 31, 2022; published electronically November 14, 2022.  \\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/21M1449646\\n\\n**Funding:** The first author’s research was supported by EPSRC via a Doctoral Training Partnership grant to the University of Warwick. The third author’s research was supported by the French Agency for Research under the ASSK project (ANR-18-CE40-0025). The fifth author’s research was supported by the Simons Foundation via award 636113.\\n\\n†Mathematics Institute, University of Warwick, Coventry, CV4 7AL, UK (b.alecu@warwick.ac.uk, V.Lozin@warwick.ac.uk).\\n\\n‡Department of Mathematics, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32611 USA (rferguson94@ufl.edu, vatter@ufl.edu).\\n\\n§Université Clermont Auvergne, LIMOS, CNRS, Aubière, 63000, France (mamadou.kante@uca.fr).\\n\\n¶Department of Computer Science, University of Liverpool, Liverpool, L69 3BX, UK (Viktor.Zamaraev@liverpool.ac.uk).\\n\\nDownloaded 11/16/22 to 35.176.47.6. Redistribution subject to SIAM license or copyright; see https://epubs.siam.org/terms-privacy\\n\\n2774\\n\\nCopyright © by SIAM. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMatroids are not Ehrhart positive (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.04465)\\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Title: Matroids are not Ehrhart positive ... In this article we disprove the conjectures asserting the positivity of the coefficients of the Ehrhart polynomial of matroid polytopes by De Loera, Haws and K\\\\\\\"oppe (2007) and of generalized permutohedra by Castillo and Liu (2015).\\nTitle: Matroids are not Ehrhart positive\\nAuthors: Luis Ferroni\\nDate: Mon May 10 16:00:43 2021\\n\\nIn this article we disprove the conjectures asserting the positivity of the coefficients of the Ehrhart polynomial of matroid polytopes by De Loera, Haws and K\\\\\\\"oppe (2007) and of generalized permutohedra by Castillo and Liu (2015). We prove constructively that for every $n\\\\geq 19$ there exist connected matroids on $n$ elements that are not Ehrhart positive. Also, we prove that for every $k\\\\geq 3$ there exist connected matroids of rank $k$ that are not Ehrhart positive. Our proofs rely on our previous results on the geometric interpretation of the operation of circuit-hyperplane relaxation and our formulas for the Ehrhart polynomials of hypersimplices and minimal matroids. This allows us to give a precise expression for the Ehrhart polynomials of all sparse paving matroids, a class of matroids which is conjectured to be predominant and which contains the counterexamples arising from our construction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMatroids are not Ehrhart positive (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2022.108337)\\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; This family can be described as the set of all (positive) Minkowski sums of dilations of standard simplices that satisfy a certain modularity property; however, in spite of being very general, it does not contain the class of matroid polytopes as a subfamily, as is pointed out in [2]. ... There exists a connected matroid 𝖬 of cardinality 20, rank 9, having 159562 bases, such that the base polytope 𝒫⁡(𝖬) is not Ehrhart positive.\\n## [10]\\n\\nLet 𝖬 be a matroid and 𝒫⁡(𝖬) be its base polytope. Then 𝒫⁡(𝖬) is Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nGeneralizing the notion of matroid to that of polymatroid allows to construct a broader class of polytopes, which correspond to the base polytopes of polymatroids. Polymatroids were introduced by Edmonds and Rota [14] and have proven useful to address many important problems in combinatorial optimization and algebraic combinatorics. In [36] Postnikov studied certain polytopes arising as deformations of permutohedra, which he named “generalized permutohedra”. Later it was realized that generalized permutohedra and base polytopes of polymatroids are (up to a translation) the same objects.\\n\\nIn 2015 [7, Conjecture 1.2] Castillo and Liu posed the following conjecture, which is an extension of Conjecture 1.1 to all generalized permutohedra.\\n\\nConjecture 1.2\\n\\n## [7]\\n\\nIf 𝒫 is an integral generalized permutohedron, then 𝒫 is Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nThere was much evidence to support these two conjectures. In what follows we give a brief overview of some known results backing up these two assertions. In [10] De Loera, Haws and Köppe computed the Ehrhart polynomials of an extensive list of base and independence polytopes of matroids and polymatroids and in all cases the coefficients were observed to be positive. Also, they were able to prove that rank 2 uniform matroids were Ehrhart positive; this result was later extended by Ferroni in [17], proving that uniform matroids of all ranks are Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nTheorem 1.3\\n\\n## [17]\\n\\nIf 𝖬 is a uniform matroid, then its base polytope 𝒫⁡(𝖬) is Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nIn [18] Ferroni used this result to show that independence polytopes of uniform matroids are Ehrhart positive as well. In [36] Postnikov gave a proof of the fact that all the members of a quite large family of generalized permutohedra, which he called “𝒴-generalized permutohedra”, were Ehrhart positive. \\nA different proof of the positivity of the linear coefficient was independently found by Jochemko and Ravichandran in [25]. It is worth mentioning that the positivity of the linear term was a particularly important clue, since most of the known examples of polytopes with at least one negative Ehrhart coefficient do have a negative linear term.\\n\\nObserve that from Castillo and Liu's result, it follows that a generalized permutohedron of dimension 𝑑 ≤5 is automatically Ehrhart positive. For example, for 𝑑= 5, the coefficients of degree 1, 2 and 3 are covered by Theorem 1.4 and the coefficients of degree 0, 4 and 5 are covered by (1). They were able to extend this result to all generalized permutohedra of dimension 𝑑 ≤6.\\n\\nHowever, in spite of all this evidence, in the present article we will disprove Conjecture 1.1 and thus also Conjecture 1.2. A related conjecture that will also be disproved is the lower bound part of [19, Conjecture 1.5]. We will construct explicit examples of matroids that fail to have positive Ehrhart coefficients. Thus, we definitely answer on the negative the Ehrhart positivity question for matroids and generalized permutohedra.\\n\\nOutline and main results\\n\\nIn Section 2 we describe the basic properties of matroids, with special emphasis on the notion of circuit-hyperplane relaxation and its relation with the class of sparse paving matroids. In Section 3 we analyze the geometric counterpart of the results and definitions described in Section 2, we also discuss the equivalence between generalized permutohedra and base polytopes of polymatroids. In Section 4 we review the Ehrhart theory of uniform and minimal matroids and we give an explicit formula for the Ehrhart polynomial of all sparse paving matroids. In Section 5 we use a result from coding theory and the formula for sparse paving matroids to construct a counterexample to Conjecture 1.1.\\n\\nTheorem 1.5\\n\\nThere exists a connected matroid 𝖬 of cardinality 20, rank 9, having 159562 bases, such that the base polytope 𝒫⁡(𝖬) is not Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nMoreover, we give an explicit description of this matroid. In Section 6 we extend this construction to higher dimensions.\\n\\nTheorem 1.6\\n\\nFor every 𝑛 ≥19 there exists a connected matroid 𝖬 on n elements such that 𝒫⁡(𝖬) is not Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nWe also prove that we can find counterexamples to Conjecture 1.1 for all ranks greater than or equal to 3.\\n\\nTheorem 1.7\\n\\nFor every 𝑘 ≥3 and n sufficiently large there exists a connected matroid 𝖬 of rank k and cardinality n such that 𝒫⁡(𝖬) is not Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nWe describe precise lower bounds for n (depending on k) that show that for example if 𝑛 ≥3589 there is always a matroid of rank 3 and cardinality n that is not Ehrhart positive.\\n\\nIn Section 7 we discuss the rank 2 case and sketch how to prove that all sparse paving matroids of rank 2 are Ehrhart positive. We finish the paper in Section 8, where we discuss some relevant facts and some extra examples.\\n\\n## 3. Matroid polytopes, polymatroids and generalized permutohedra\\n\\nIn this section we will review some definitions and fundamental facts about matroid polytopes, polymatroids and generalized permutohedra.\\n\\n### 3.1. Matroid polytopes\\n\\nOne of the many features of matroids is that they can be studied using tools from different areas of mathematics. In particular, starting from a matroid one can construct a polytope.\\n\\nDefinition 3.1\\n\\nLet 𝖬= (𝐸,ℬ) be a matroid on 𝐸= {1,…,𝑛}. For each 1 ≤𝑖 ≤𝑛, let us call 𝑒𝑖 the i-th canonical vector in ℝ𝑛. For every 𝐴 ⊆𝐸 define 𝑒𝐴=∑𝑖∈𝐴𝑒𝑖∈ℝ𝑛. The base polytope of 𝖬 is defined as 𝒫⁡(𝖬)=convex hull{𝑒𝐵:𝐵∈ℬ}⊆ℝ𝑛.\\n\\nIf instead of taking the convex hull of the indicator vectors of the bases of the matroid one considers all independent subsets, the resulting polytope 𝒫𝐼⁡(𝖬) is customarily called the independence polytope of 𝖬. The study of matroids from the polyhedral point of view was initiated by Edmonds in [13], and over the past half century has been a prominent area of research in diverse areas of mathematics such as optimization, discrete geometry or tropical geometry.\\n\\nRemark 3.2\\n\\nSince all the bases of a matroid have cardinality equal to the rank of the matroid, 𝑘= rk⁡(𝐸), we see that the base polytope has all of its vertices lying on the hyperplane ∑𝑛𝑖=1𝑥𝑖= 𝑘 in ℝ𝑛. Hence, its dimension is at most 𝑛− 1. In [16] Feichtner and Sturmfels proved that dim⁡𝒫⁡(𝖬)=𝑛−𝑐⁡(𝖬), where 𝑐⁡(𝖬) is the number of connected components of the matroid 𝖬. In particular a matroid on n elements is connected if and only if dim⁡𝒫⁡(𝖬)= 𝑛−1. Also, since 𝒫⁡(𝖬1 ⊕𝖬2)= 𝒫⁡(𝖬1)×𝒫⁡(𝖬2), and the Ehrhart polynomial of a product of polytopes is just the product of their Ehrhart polynomials, we can restrict ourselves only to connected matroids.\\n\\nRemark 3.3\\n\\nNotice that if 𝖬 is a matroid of rank k and cardinality n, then 𝖬⁎ is a matroid of rank 𝑛− 𝑘 and cardinality n. Moreover, the polytopes 𝒫⁡(𝖬) and 𝒫⁡(𝖬⁎) are obtained one from the other via an involution of the form 𝑥 ↦(1,…,1)− 𝑥. In particular, if we denote by ehr⁡(𝖬,𝑡) the Ehrhart polynomial of 𝒫⁡(𝖬) and analogously for 𝖬⁎, it is clear that ehr⁡(𝖬,𝑡)=ehr⁡(𝖬⁎,𝑡).\\n\\nTherefore, 𝖬 is a counterexample to Conjecture 1.1 if and only if 𝖬⁎ is a counterexample too. Hence, it suffices to look at matroids satisfying 𝑘 ≤𝑛− 𝑘 or, equivalently, 2⁢𝑘 ≤𝑛.\\n\\nObserve that base polytopes of matroids are 0/1-polytopes. This, together with a property about the edges characterizes them, due to a fundamental result by Gel'fand, Goresky, MacPherson and Serganova in [21].\\n\\nTheorem 3.4\\n\\n## [21]\\n\\nLet 𝒫 be a polytope in ℝ𝑛. Then 𝒫 is the base polytope of a matroid if and only if the following two conditions hold:\\n\\n  * •\\n\\n𝒫 is a 0/1-polytope.\\n\\n  * •\\n\\nAll the edges of 𝒫 are of the form 𝑒𝑖− 𝑒𝑗 for 𝑖 ≠𝑗.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOn the relationship between letter graphs and geometric grid classes of permutations (https://livrepository.liverpool.ac.uk/3130005/1/2107.03447v1.pdf)\\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; In this paper, we uncover a connection between two seemingly unrelated notions: lettericity, from structural graph theory, and geometric griddability, from the world of permutation patterns.\\nLetter Graphs and Geometric Grid Classes of Permutations\\n\\nBogdan Alecu *  \\nMathematics Institute  \\nUniversity of Warwick  \\nCoventry, UK\\n\\nRobert Ferguson  \\nDepartment of Mathematics  \\nUniversity of Florida  \\nGainesville, Florida USA\\n\\nMamadou Moustapha Kanté†  \\nUniversité Clermont Auvergne  \\nLIMOS, CNRS  \\nAubière, France\\n\\nVadim Lozin  \\nMathematics Institute  \\nUniversity of Warwick  \\nCoventry, UK\\n\\nVincent Vatter‡  \\nDepartment of Mathematics  \\nUniversity of Florida  \\nGainesville, Florida USA\\n\\nViktor Zamaraev  \\nDepartment of Computer Science  \\nUniversity of Liverpool  \\nLiverpool, UK\\n\\nJuly 9, 2021\\n\\nIn this paper, we uncover a connection between two seemingly unrelated notions: lettericity, from structural graph theory, and geometric griddability, from the world of permutation patterns. Both of these notions capture important structural properties of their respective classes of objects. We prove that these notions are equivalent in the sense that a permutation class is geometrically griddable if and only if the corresponding class of inversion graphs has bounded lettericity.\\n\\n1. INTRODUCTION\\n\\nStructural graph theory and the study of permutation patterns are two flourishing areas of mathematics. They share a few conceptual similarities—among others, the widespread featuring of forbidden substructures, and the prominent usage of decompositions. Nevertheless, the fields have developed their own separate terminologies, techniques, and goals.\\n\\nBecause of this, it happens periodically that equivalent notions appear in the two areas, independently of each other, and under different names. In most cases, a relationship between such notions can be derived directly from their definitions. Sometimes, however, this link can be much more subtle. This is indeed the case with the two notions studied in the present paper: lettericity on the one hand, and geometric griddability on the other. At a first reading of the definitions, nothing suggests that there should be a connection between these two notions. However, such a connection\\n\\n* Alecu’s research was supported by EPSRC via a Doctoral Training Partnership grant to the University of Warwick.  \\n† Kanté’s research was supported by the French Agency for Research under the ASSK project (ANR-18-CE40-0025).  \\n‡ Vatter’s research was supported by the Simons Foundation via award number 636113.\\n\\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nLETTER GRAPHS AND GEOMETRIC GRID CLASSES OF PERMUTATIONS (https://livrepository.liverpool.ac.uk/3168890/)\\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; We uncover a connection between two seemingly unrelated notions: lettericity, from structural graph theory, and geometric griddability, from the world of permutation patterns.\\n# LETTER GRAPHS AND GEOMETRIC GRID CLASSES OF PERMUTATIONS\\n\\n* * *\\n\\nAlecu, B, Ferguson, R, Kante, MM, Lozin, VV, Vatter, V and Zamaraev, V ORCID: 0000-0001-5755-4141 (2022) LETTER GRAPHS AND GEOMETRIC GRID CLASSES OF PERMUTATIONS SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics, 36 (4). pp. 2774-2797. ISSN 0895-4801, 1095-7146\\n\\nAccess the full-text of this item by clicking on the Open Access link.\\n\\nOfficial URL: https://doi.org/10.1137/21m1449646\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nWe uncover a connection between two seemingly unrelated notions: lettericity, from structural graph theory, and geometric griddability, from the world of permutation patterns. Both of these notions capture important structural properties of their respective classes of objects. We prove that these notions are equivalent in the sense that a permutation class is geometrically griddable if and only if the corresponding class of inversion graphs has bounded lettericity.\\n\\nItem Type:  | Article\\nUncontrolled Keywords:  | inversion graphs, permutation patterns, well-quasi-order\\nDivisions:  | Faculty of Science & Engineering > School of Electrical Engineering, Electronics and Computer Science\\nDepositing User:  | Symplectic Admin\\nDate Deposited:  | 09 Mar 2023 10:28\\nLast Modified:  | 28 Feb 2026 23:40\\nDOI:  | 10.1137/21M1449646\\nOpen Access URL:  | https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.03447.pdf\\nRelated Websites:  | \\n\\n  * Author\\n  * Publisher\\nURI:  | https://livrepository.liverpool.ac.uk/id/eprint/3168890--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nWRAP_Coversheet_Theses_new (https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/163771/1/WRAP_Theses_Alecu_2021.pdf)\\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 4.7 years ago; # Lettericity and geometric griddability\\n\\nIn this chapter, we will study two notions – letter graph representations [Pet02], from the world of graphs, and geometric grid classes [Alb+13], from the world of permutations.\\n# Chapter 3\\n# Lettericity and geometric griddability\\n\\nIn this chapter, we will study two notions – letter graph representations [Pet02], from the world of graphs, and geometric grid classes [Alb+13], from the world of permutations. We will see that, despite being defined in seemingly unrelated ways, the two notions have a close relationship between them. This stems from the fact that they capture the same structural data of their respective combinatorial objects: a partition of their elements into simple “bags”, and a linear ordering of the elements interacting nicely with this partition.\\n\\nIn Section 3.1, we introduce the necessary terminology and notation. Section 3.2 presents the results published in our paper [Ale+20b]. We prove a conjecture from that paper in Section 3.3, and finally, Section 3.4 provides a discussion about a further direction of research.\\n\\n## 3.1 Letter graphs and griddability: preliminaries\\n\\n### 3.1.1 Letter graphs and lettericity\\n\\nThe notion of letter graphs was introduced in [Pet02]. Our terminology differs only superficially from the one used there. We will need some basic notions from the theory of formal languages; rather than defining them separately in the most general setting possible, we will introduce the relevant definitions as we go along, and adapt them to our restricted setting.\\n\\nOur starting point is a finite digraph \\\\(\\\\mathcal{D} = (\\\\Sigma, A)\\\\) that we will call the *decoding digraph*, or simply *decoder*. We call \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\) a (finite) *alphabet*, and we refer to its elements (that is, the vertices of \\\\(\\\\mathcal{D}\\\\)) as *letters* (or *symbols*). Now let \\\\(\\\\Sigma^*\\\\) be the set of finite\\n\\n14--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMatroids are not Ehrhart positive - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2105.04465v3)\\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Matroids are not Ehrhart positive ... In this article we disprove the conjectures asserting the positivity of the coefficients of the Ehrhart polynomial of matroid polytopes by De Loera, Haws and Köppe (2007) and of generalized permutohedra by Castillo and Liu (2015).\\n\\n## Matroids are not Ehrhart positive\\n\\nLuis Ferroni\\n\\nPublished: 2021-05-10 | Updated: 2022-03-07\\n\\nComments: 22 pages, final version. Accepted in Advances in Mathematics\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nDOI: 10.1016/j.aim.2022.108337\\n\\nconnected matroid ehrhart polynomial matroid matroid polytope paving matroid permutohedra polytope sparse paving matroid polynomial\\n\\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nIn this article we disprove the conjectures asserting the positivity of the coefficients of the Ehrhart polynomial of matroid polytopes by De Loera, Haws and Köppe (2007) and of generalized permutohedra by Castillo and Liu (2015). We prove constructively that for every $n\\\\geq 19$ there exist connected matroids on $n$ elements that are not Ehrhart positive. Also, we prove that for every $k\\\\geq 3$ there exist connected matroids of rank $k$ that are not Ehrhart positive. Our proofs rely on our previous results on the geometric interpretation of the operation of circuit-hyperplane relaxation and our formulas for the Ehrhart polynomials of hypersimplices and minimal matroids. This allows us to give a precise expression for the Ehrhart polynomials of all sparse paving matroids, a class of matroids which is conjectured to be predominant and which contains the counterexamples arising from our construction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGEOMETRIC GRID CLASSES AND LETTERICITY (https://sites.cs.st-andrews.ac.uk/pp25/program/booklet-pp25.pdf)\\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Though initially appearing unrelated, geometric griddability and lettericity share deep structural similarities.\\nGEOMETRIC GRID CLASSES AND LETTERICITY\\n\\nVince Vatter                                                                 University of Florida\\n\\nThis talk is based on joint work with Bogdan Alecu, Robert Ferguson, Mamadou Moustapha Kanté, Vadim Lozin, Sean Mandrick, and Viktor Zamaraev\\n\\nGeometric grid classes of permutations [1], are defined using matrices whose entries indicate monotone segments in specified grid cells. Given a matrix, its geometric grid class includes all permutations whose plots can be “drawn on” the corresponding grid figure.\\n\\nLettericity, introduced by Petkovšek [2], is a graph parameter defined via representations known as letter graphs. A letter graph is constructed from a word over a finite alphabet together with a decoder—a specified set of letter pairs determining adjacency. Vertices correspond to letters of the word, and edges between vertices are dictated by the decoder. The lettericity of a graph is the smallest alphabet size needed for such a representation.\\n\\nThough initially appearing unrelated, geometric griddability and lettericity share deep structural similarities. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\niv                                                                 INTRODUCTION (https://amsdottorato.unibo.it/id/eprint/9945/1/FerroniRivetti_Luis_tesi.pdf)\\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; They are mainly related to *independence matroid polytopes* ... polytopes that are not Ehrhart positive.\\nA.\\n  178:14, 2021.\\n\\n- L. Ferroni, *On the Ehrhart polynomial of minimal matroids*, Discrete Comput.\\n  Geom., 2021.\\n\\n- L. Ferroni, *Integer point enumeration on independence polytopes and half-open\\n  hypersimplices*, Discrete Math., 344(8):112446, 2021.\\n\\n- L. Ferroni, *Matroids are not Ehrhart positive*, submitted.\\n\\n- L. Ferroni, K. Jochemko, B. Schröter, *Ehrhart polynomials of rank two matroids*,\\n  preprint.\\n\\n    We have included in this thesis some (few) additional results that do not appear\\nwithin the above articles. They are mainly related to *independence matroid polytopes*\\nand their Ehrhart theory. For instance, we show that there exist independence matroid\\npolytope